{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting","id":804011,"title":"Why Solars Can't Change the Setting","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Solar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-08T14:37:46-08:00","id":804011,"title":"Why Solars Can't Change the Setting","content":"Full disclosure: the title is clickbait. Solars can indeed change the setting, but not by means of Setting-Changing Prana. I've seen a few posts and threads over the last few months asking where the charms are to do big stuff. What does a Solar need to do to cure a lot of sick people, build a lot of stuff, or manage an organization or state? What do we roll, and how can we enhance the roll?<br /> <br /> I went back to the &quot;What we know&quot; wiki and found some stuff John said on the matter:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Macro magic doesn't make sense. The Solars will roll up the setting in a rug and carry it off within a year and nobody will be able to stop them, most especially other Exalts. The CRM is about macro magic, about ignoring personal, individual conflicts and abstracting those things into a dice roll. The biggest obstacle to a Solar's ascent is that he is one supernal mind/spirit among millions of mortals with various flaws, blindspots, failings, weaknesses, etc., and he has to work with and through these people to get things done. You can't make running a nation a matter of rolling his dice and using his Charms, because:<br /> <br /> 1) The Solar disappears completely on the macro scale.<br /> 2) The Solar's stats and Charms appended onto an organization makes that organization a Solar, and unstoppable except by another Solar or Exalt with comparable powers.<br /> 3) #2 entails erasing the obstacles and the roleplaying involved in this process. It also doesn't make sense. Solar Charms enhance their own actions. It smashes the setting into flinders when their Charms also enhance the actions of thousands of other people. While Exalted is an extremely high powered game, it's not okay to publish systems that make the entire setting implausible.<br /> <br /> The net result is that you stop playing Exalted and start playing a purely mechanical system that projects advancement that is wildly, hysterically disproportionate to how fast an Exalt-managed organization actually advances. I will never put a dot on your character sheet that says &quot;and when you buy this one, you have successfully resurrected the First Age. Hooray for you.&quot; </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Think about it: Exalted is about playing an Exalt. If you don't want to actually roll out what your subordinates are doing because it would detract away from playing your Exalt, I'm not going to make a system to abstract doing exactly that. No abstraction would make sense or have any bearing on your character.<br /> <br /> Your character can influence, inspire, and unite people. He can educate them, arm them, and train them for battle. We have written rules to reflect these things in the corebook. But there isn't actually a roll to decide whether they succeed or fail because no such roll would make sense and no such system would be calibrated to the actual rate of progress an Exalt should be able to push.<br /> <br /> There are very real reasons wars are lost, projects fail or succeed, etc., and these instances can be recreated in Exalted and exploited by your Charms, and that is what I am out to reveal in EX3. </div> </div> </div>Some people have pointed out that we could use more information about the kinds of obstacles there are to achieving big goals, and I think that makes some sense. While there are resources outside Exalted for learning more, it would be nice for future books to help us figure out what characters in Creation might have to overcome.<br /> <br /> What we won't see, and what is far from an accidental omission, is a way to make Creation really nice to live in really fast. When large scale change is a matter of rolling dice, the Solar will succeed unless something bigger stands in her way. With that in mind, we may as well skip the bit about the dice.<br /> <br /> So what's bigger than a Solar in this context? Time, finite resources, and other people, for starters. The Solar doctor can drag even the frailest patient back from the brink of death, but she may not be able to save a village from the plague even if she pushes herself to the limits. A Solar craftsman can quickly produce arms and armor for his circle, but not for a legion of peasant conscripts. A Solar cannot run a state without regard for the physical or social makeup of the place or the people; she cannot force others to succeed or create prosperity out of nothing with no means other than her essence and her will (OK, WST maybe breaks some rules there - no wonder it's such a big deal).<br /> <br /> Whatever was achieved in the First Age certainly depended on the Solars and their magic, but it was not an inevitable result of their existence. And however the Solars shape the current Age, they will still have to try, and they may err in more than their aims and they may fail for more reasons than not enough dice or not the right charms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":23}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T14:47:29-08:00","id":804016,"title":"","content":"I came here with a pitchfork and left thoughtful","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Exalted: Princes of the Universe<br /> A Third Edition Exalted Podcast<br /> <b><u><a href=\"http://atrusilk.podbean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://atrusilk.podbean.com/</a></u></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8769-tavelgorge","name":"TavelGorge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"498"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:02:00-08:00","id":804035,"title":"","content":"I agree that &quot;my organisation has Solar Charms&quot; basically makes them unstoppable and that would be bad.<br /> <br /> <i>If you don't want to actually roll out what your subordinates are doing because it would detract away from playing your Exalt, I'm not going to make a system to abstract doing exactly that. No abstraction would make sense or have any bearing on your character.</i><br /> <br /> <br /> This, on the other hand... geopolitics is HARD to GM. At the scale where &quot;the Solar disappears&quot;, well, the GM still needs to say what's going on, and I dislike the answer to &quot;what happens&quot; being &quot;meh make some shit up&quot;. Knowing what your choices led to (rather than knowing that because the game needs a plot, bad things were inevitable) is... important.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/234-superg","name":"SuperG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar234_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"451"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:04:45-08:00","id":804042,"title":"","content":"To be fair 'Bad things are inevitable' is kind of the only single truth when it comes to geo politics though","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Exalted: Princes of the Universe<br /> A Third Edition Exalted Podcast<br /> <b><u><a href=\"http://atrusilk.podbean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://atrusilk.podbean.com/</a></u></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8769-tavelgorge","name":"TavelGorge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"498"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:21:12-08:00","id":804052,"title":"","content":"I doubt this is adding much to this thread, but that makes more sense when read with Root's framing than it would otherwise. And yeah, I find it convincing, esp point #2 if I understand correctly - representing the Solars' capabilities as applied through large groups would mean macro-scale effects which would be either like JM describes (the group is effectively a Solar and cuts through the setting way too quickly) or (the alternative JM doesn't talk about) would try to realistically model the difficulties of layers of people and limits on time and space, and so be both weak and slow, through lots of modifying conditions that would ultimately end up being as much of an abstraction (so fail to feel very &quot;Solar&quot; and impactful and show the Solar hero).<br /> <br /> Though it did make me think that there are macro effects that seems like it could have macro scale effects on very large groups in setting: Sorcery. So to what extent is the macro scale effect thing applicable to sorcery, and if so, to what extent is that &quot;Not a problem. It's freaking sorcery; it's both supposed to do that, and its methods and style inherently mitigate any of the negatives discussed which applicable to other macro scale magic&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:33:35-08:00","id":804062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Root</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting#post804011\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some people have pointed out that we could use more information about the kinds of obstacles there are to achieving big goals, and I think that makes some sense. While there are resources outside Exalted for learning more, it would be nice for future books to help us figure out what characters in Creation might have to overcome.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuperG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804035#post804035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This, on the other hand... geopolitics is HARD to GM. At the scale where &quot;the Solar disappears&quot;, well, the GM still needs to say what's going on, and I dislike the answer to &quot;what happens&quot; being &quot;meh make some shit up&quot;. Knowing what your choices led to (rather than knowing that because the game needs a plot, bad things were inevitable) is... important.</div> </div> </div> </div>These are the things I am most interested in. Not Charms that make resolving these kinds of challenges automatic, but advice and tools for Storytellers and players who want to tell stories about their characters struggling with the difficulties of geopolitics and running/playing the game on an institutional scale. I am all for this, and it's the kind of thing that the game is supposed to be about - has been from the very beginning! It's super exciting! It's also incredibly daunting, and we really need some reference material that provides a place to start and a basic framework that is designed with the game of Exalted in mind.<br /> <br /> I don't know what that would look like, but I do know that I for one would really love something more substantial than, say, a recommendation to go read Machiavelli and Gibbon. I can do that, and it's probably good advice in general, but it's not actually what I need to help me run a game of Exalted.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Share your wonders in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop#post823884\" target=\"_blank\">The Artifact and Evocation Workshop</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","name":"Ferryman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"960"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:39:53-08:00","id":804064,"title":"","content":"hmmm, there are a few ways i can think of that can put spanners in the wheels of solars plans.<br /> <br /> examples, a solar going for a huge trading company will inevitable start stepping on the toes of the guild, and the guild have dealt with exalts LOADS of times before and is still purely governed by mortals.<br /> Whilst the guild may lack the power a solar posses, they do posses numbers. Thaumaturges, the occasional god, god-blooded, hired or forced exalts, a nearly infinite supply of mercenaries and their most powerful weapon, most markets in creation.<br /> What can your trade company do if you've gotten embargoed on all your goods? hire mercs to harass the guild? yeah, good luck with that since the guild will probably buy them out from under you or just send in their own mercenaries. If you are still a threat to them they WILL find out who you where and who you hold dear and then they will use them to defeat you because they don't care if they have to kill of an entire city to beat you, if they see you as a big enough threat they will!<br /> <br /> Loyalist abyssals.<br /> they have a functioning infrastructure that you can't really do much against since well, the underworld is a bit more tricky to manipulate for a solar than creation is.<br /> they are good at breaking down organizations and they will do that, not to mention the fact that if your caravans keep getting ambushed by hungry ghosts and anyone that buys your goods get's eaten by ghosts, well no one will want to buy your stuff then!<br /> They also have the power of their deathlord to back them up and if your soldiers kill a specter, it doesn't really matter to them, they will just reform, one solar is still just one person, a solar can't be everywhere at once and every single one of your employees that fall increases the abyssals power.<br /> they can also manipulate ancestor worshipers by making their ancestors just go NOPE to your goods.<br /> <br /> the realm, well a similar situation to the other two, they have power and would greatly limit the amount of people you could sell stuff to, especially if the immaculates get instructed to hunt down your cargo, because there are few people willing to make an enemy of the realm.<br /> <br /> Lunars, well luna is a tricky goddess/god and is her chosen do posses a lot of deceiving power, so yeah they can buy up a lot of your goods with things like rocks that seemed like jade talents when you got them.<br /> <br /> well there's a few that i can think of, I hope i made sense and understood the thread correctly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm making the Tales of Exalts webcomic<br /> <a href=\"http://xanroth.deviantart.com/gallery/50854781/tales-of-exalts\" target=\"_blank\">http://xanroth.deviantart.com/galler...ales-of-exalts</a><br /> alchemical report <a href=\"http://xanroth.deviantart.com/gallery/54812164/Alchemical-Report\" target=\"_blank\">http://xanroth.deviantart.com/galler...hemical-Report</a><br /> the fall <a href=\"http://xanroth.deviantart.com/gallery/55265686/the-fall\" target=\"_blank\">http://xanroth.deviantart.com/gallery/55265686/the-fall</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4455-xanroth","name":"Xanroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4455_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1072"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:43:49-08:00","id":804067,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuperG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804035#post804035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">geopolitics is HARD to GM.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wonder...<br /> <br /> Is that a difficulty of &quot;this is a thing one feels the need to do, despite lacking much know-how and enthusiasm&quot; or...<br /> <br /> Darn it, I've got my conclusion, but I'm struggling to articulate a path that leads up to it that doesn't come across as antagonistic or condescending.<br /> <br /> I suppose my point is... if getting into an intricate portrayal of such is difficult for a given Storyteller, maybe they're just better off going for something more... streamlined. I don't mean any judgement in that; streamlined is good. Just...<br /> <br /> Aaarrggghh, this is very frustrating. The thing I'm trying to work backwards from is another reference to Star Wars; where they were able to make the idea that there were deeper political implications to character actions and the story in general the province of about two lines (&quot;Holding her is dangerous; if word of this gets out, it might generate sympathy for the Rebellion in the Senate.&quot; &quot;The Imperial Senate will no longer be of any concern to us. I have just received word that the Emperor has dissolved the council permanently. The last remnants of the Old Republic have been swept away forever... The regional governors now have direct control over their territories. Fear will keep the local systems in line. Fear of this battle station.&quot;) while barely needing to be a component of the actual plot.<br /> <br /> Does that make sense? Is that enough to make what I'm trying to get at apparent?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:44:36-08:00","id":804069,"title":"","content":"Any help to arm a group with the ability to run a game of thrones type political wheel game would be to publish competent and cohesive books on the setting and how it all fits together. This writer team is the team that makes those books. Like masters of jade and all that jazz.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Exalted: Princes of the Universe<br /> A Third Edition Exalted Podcast<br /> <b><u><a href=\"http://atrusilk.podbean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://atrusilk.podbean.com/</a></u></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8769-tavelgorge","name":"TavelGorge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"498"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:46:30-08:00","id":804070,"title":"","content":"Solars <i>can</i> outfit legions of troops and save villages from plague using their charms and abilities, though.<br /> <br /> Scale isn't by any means an impossible barrier for Solars to overcome. A Solar King's foreign policy can be &quot;don't mess with my city, even though it is militarily weak, because I am strong enough to hack my way through an army and kill you, and I've done it before.&quot; That's eminently doable with the rules. I'd be surprised if, in the upcoming game I am running, there won't be a moment when a critical military action which would be given to a thousand veteran troops is given to the Dawn, because he really is that terrifying an engine of destruction. The scale of the opposition is overcome by raw individual power.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I think this is a bit of an example of Exalted getting het up over something that other games don't see the need to worry about. Of course Solar's are relevant on a macro scale because of their charms, that's clear from just reading some of the charms, especially at higher essence levels. And that's before we even get into the potential of Sorcery. Desert province with perpetually little food due to lack of arable land? Good thing that this Essence 5 Twilight is here to cast Benediction of Archgenesis then! Solars go as deep into scale as they do into individual perfection, in many cases, and better than almost any other Exalted too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:58:48-08:00","id":804078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Solar King's foreign policy can be &quot;don't mess with my city, even though it is militarily weak, because I am strong enough to hack my way through an army and kill you, and I've done it before.&quot; That's eminently doable with the rules.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think there's a point at which the rules have to be set aside a bit in portraying and approaching the setting; you can't really effectively use the battle group rules to portray armies of up to 30,000 troops or something. There's a point where people start interacting with assumptions of things like geopolitics that are very similar to the real world. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The scale of the opposition is overcome by raw individual power.</div> </div> </div> </div> Even when that scale is so large that, in the time it takes you to get from one end of a full-scale military campaign to another, several cities have been devastated? <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solars are relevant on a macro scale because of their charms, that's clear from just reading some of the charms, especially at higher essence levels.</div> </div> </div> </div> What do you have in mind? <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this is a bit of an example of Exalted getting het up over something that other games don't see the need to worry about.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's part of the draw to some people, isn't it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:07:16-08:00","id":804087,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804052#post804052\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Though it did make me think that there are macro effects that seems like it could have macro scale effects on very large groups in setting: Sorcery. So to what extent is the macro scale effect thing applicable to sorcery, and if so, to what extent is that &quot;Not a problem. It's freaking sorcery; it's both supposed to do that, and its methods and style inherently mitigate any of the negatives discussed which applicable to other macro scale magic&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this is why I like the XP component to workings. A sorcerer can't just keep cranking out fair weather and good soil everywhere she goes without paying a heavy price. Those rarest sorcerers with the power to deeply change wide swathes of the setting might lack the desire to pay the cost or to draw so much attention to themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804067#post804067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I suppose my point is... if getting into an intricate portrayal of such is difficult for a given Storyteller, maybe they're just better off going for something more... streamlined. I don't mean any judgement in that; streamlined is good. Just...</div> </div> </div> </div>Rougher guidelines, like the ones in the core, work well when people are OK with the limits of their knowledge. Like, maybe you want to build a navy, so the ST checks his notes and says there are a lot of trees in your region, so go for it. Maybe someone googles &quot;Spanish Armada&quot; to see if they can get a time frame for building a fleet, and that feels pretty right. If the character's city depended on the lumber industry or if the fleet gets destroyed real fast, there could be some interesting wrinkles. But not always.<br /> <br /> For another group it might be more satisfying to talk about where the workforce comes from, how everything is funded, whether the infrastructure supports this project, whether the harbor is deep enough for large warships, who trains the sailors, how this impacts the harvest and other elements of the economy, and so on. But I don't know if everyone needs to go that route, because it might feel less like the core experience of Exalted.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:25:32-08:00","id":804095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804078#post804078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I think there's a point at which the rules have to be set aside a bit in portraying and approaching the setting; you can't really effectively use the battle group rules to portray armies of up to 30,000 troops or something. There's a point where people start interacting with assumptions of things like geopolitics that are very similar to the real world. Even when that scale is so large that, in the time it takes you to get from one end of a full-scale military campaign to another, several cities have been devastated? What do you have in mind? That's part of the draw to some people, isn't it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I am fine with the rules not being treated as laws of physics, totally. But if I turn around to the group, in the game I am playing, and say &quot;no you can't just treat the Dawn like he can fight a thousand elite troops by himself before breakfast, and then go on to engage various further en&quot; then they're going to be a bit confused, because he can do that and they've seen it happen! If a mortal prince and a thousand elite troops turn up, then various stripes of Solars can engage that <i>by themselves</i> through sorcery, sheer personal might and such. While it's okay for there to be a difference between the capabilities of the PCs and the way the setting works in the minds of the people in it, a Solar <i>can do those things</i>. Explicitly, in the rules!<br /> <br /> And sure, you can say that there are situations where a lone hero is outclassed by the nature as a lone hero. Same time, though, a Solar can summon an army of Demons or Elementals. A Solar can run so fast that they can engage multiple armies by themselves, in order, faster than an entire mortal army could! Much faster! Or, like, use an artifact to fly there! Or a spell I mean sure if you arbitrarily increase the scale up and up and up it becomes more difficult (a Solar will find it difficult to fight, like, four armies at once in four different places) but certainly a Solar can deal with problems of scale <i>to a point</i> in the same way that they can deal with problems of excellence <i>to a point</i> (A Solar can't fight four armies in four different places in the same way that a Solar can't just one shot Farakhu, they aren't powerful enough to do that, but they are powerful enough to do a lot below that which makes them individually relevant on a national level) For an example, I point to; a Dawn fighting against a Magnitude 5 Battle Group (several times in a row), or a Sorcerer casting Benediction of Archgenesis to literally change the economic circumstances of an entire civilisation through <i>one spell</i>, which are two examples I've already given, God King's Shrike off the top of my head, the higher essence bureaucracy and presence charms can completely alter existing institutions, belief systems and so on through the correct application of charm to face, though I can't be bothered to search too deep into them for more specific charm names right now.<br /> <br /> But yeah, I think we are getting different ideas about what I am saying the fan base gets het up about; I think another game would have cheerfully just put a bureaucracy system in there so that people can use that if they want, makes life easier and the game fun etc etc etc, and Exalted is (for me) a bit notorious in that we always have to spill many words about whether such a system fits with our own personal image of Exalted in a manner which can be charming but also gets in the way of playing the game, IMO. I think another game would just kind of accept that Solars are powerful enough to be individually relevant on a national scale if they want to because they obvious are capable of that, and so why not just roll with it?<br /> <br /> Perhaps the best way to put it; the Exalted fanbase can at times put the relevance of academic hand-wringing before the practicalities of Exalted being a game to play and have fun with. I'm a practical guy when it comes to this sort of thing, rather than discuss the concept restrictions of Solars when it comes to scale, practically speaking in the game, they can overcome scale just as well as most other things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 01-08-2016, 07:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:32:02-08:00","id":804097,"title":"","content":"I don't think anyone doubts that a Solar, given the chance and the right skills, can chew through a thousand-man army. But he can only do that if the army cheerfully engages him on those terms. If his opponent instead splits his army into five 200-man raiding parties and starts burning down villages, though, it doesn't really <i>matter </i>that the Solar could kill all one thousand of them given the chance; the Solar can't protect his kingdom, and his people will lose faith in him because of that.<br /> <br /> That's the sort of thing that organizations and bureaucracies and communications and supply lines and, yes, having an army of your own are for. The writers have made it clear that they don't think Solars should be able to cheat those things away trivially, and should instead engage meaningfully with them. And that's great! So...can we have some material to help us do that?<br /> <br /> EDIT: Maybe I can expound on what I want a little via an extended example:<br /> <br /> Say I have a player facing the above situation. He has risen through his might and prowess and is hailed as a hero in a certain region, and they now look to him for the just rule and protection that he has promised to give them because...well, that's what he did! He kicks the old ruler out and installs himself as the benevolent warlord of this province. But the (not yet dead) king-in-exile has removed himself to sanctuary in a neighboring province and sworn to reclaim his territory. Story basics: handled.<br /> <br /> Now, the king-in-exile is a canny sort and knows that the Solar can hack through any number of soldiers sent against him personally (not least because the Solar has boasted of this publicly, and done so on several occasions), so he doesn't lay siege to his old haunt where the Solar now hangs his hat. He starts sending out smaller but still dangerous raiding forces to burn crops and torch villages and in general plunder his own former subjects to undermine the Solar's rule. It works, because the Solar can't be everywhere at once. So the Solar wises up and decides he wants to raise forces of his own to patrol his lands and defend against these attacks and prove that he really can protect his people. So how does he do this?<br /> <br /> Well, first he has to recruit soldiers. No problem, lots of starry-eyed young farmers eager to be heroes, but...he needs a lot of them, and he can't do all of this personally. He needs to send out recruiters. Okay, a dozen reasonably convincing people can manage this. Also send scribes, because you should know who it is you are signing up.<br /> <br /> Okay, now you need to train them. That also means feeding and housing and outfitting them, which means supplies. You could make them, or you could buy them from the local Guild factor. That means setting up standing supply contracts. You'll need to pay for all this too. Also, probably you should start paying your employees, since you're starting to have a lot of them, too. And the soldiers, they're going to need to be paid (you don't want to end up like Caesar, after all). You could make your own equipment, but then you'll need raw materials and infrastructure to do that - did you already have all that? Where is the money for all this coming from? Shit, you're going to need to start collecting taxes and tariffs, aren't you? And that means you'll need accountants to keep track of your expenses, so that you know where the money is going.<br /> <br /> Training. Probably going to need some instructors to keep the recruits in line and drilling when the Solar isn't doing it himself, but let's assume this is something he really <i>can </i>manage. League of Iron and Tiger Warrior Training are pretty sweet, after all! Awesome, we got some <i>badasses </i>up in here. Who's going to lead them? The Solar can't be everywhere, after all - that's why he's doing all of this. Gonna need to recruit some trusted lieutenants.<br /> <br /> Huh, all this military buildup has made one of his neighbors nervous. They've sent an ambassador to establish regular diplomatic relations with the Solar's government (because he has a government now!). They'll need accommodations. Also, diplomats are basically spies, so he'll need to set some people to keep an eye on this fellow and make sure he doesn't start...doing things he shouldn't. Probably time for a spymaster too, unless the Solar wants to spend all of his time managing intelligence reports. And military reports. And the budget. And tax receipts.<br /> <br /> We're going to need some ministers, aren't we?<br /> <br /> Now, the above is just spitballing, and it's not that complicated for someone with some foundational knowledge of history, political science, and government. But here's the thing: how much of the above is the average Exalted player (or ST) expected to bring to the table with them? Because so far the game itself doesn't offer even the barest framework for players to start with. I haven't even gotten into things that are setting-specific to Creation, like how the above might be affected by the existence of spirits or magic. But even without those complications, one has to ask: if it is the position of the writers that 1) the players of Solars should be able to tell stories of conquest and empire-building, <i>and </i>2) that they don't get to cheat their way past the necessary frameworks that make such things possible, then 3) is it not reasonable to expect some advice on how to go about doing this?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Share your wonders in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop#post823884\" target=\"_blank\">The Artifact and Evocation Workshop</a>","edited":{"userid":1315,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","user":"Ferryman","date":". 01-08-2016, 08:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","name":"Ferryman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"960"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:47:41-08:00","id":804107,"title":"","content":"Root, I am consistently impressed by your posts. Very well said!<br /> <br /> Solar: In a sense, they can. They can accomplish a LOT just by virtue of what they are. But Solars are not granted infinite wisdom, foresight, or morality. Solars are granted the ability of The Unconquered Sun. Left long enough, they will surpass all limitations. But what will they use this for? This is the nature of macro vs micro in the terms of OP/the developers. It's not that scale is impossible, it's that... Well... Lets back up just a bit.<br /> <br /> In second edition, Solars had powers that could simply end a story before it was told. It wasn't just raw power, but the guidance of the sun's prowess was such that there was such a thing as perfect government running. And if your goal in storytelling was to gloss over the making of a perfected world, and instead just do the cool fight scenes? Well, sure. That's fine! But by making that the default, it meant that anyone who wanted more nuance would struggle uphill. It's much easier to decide you don't want to include a thing than it is to say you want to make something wholesale. So in third edition, the game switched stances and no longer allows solars to have charms that grant perfect judgement or story-cutting charms.<br /> <br /> But when you say things like.. a solar can outfit a leigon of peasants. A solar can cure a plague. They don't have charms that just DO that... they have charms that let them do an action supernally. You can cure this person, guaranteed, in 1 hour. How are you gonna get past that limitation? Triage, some people die, but you save the most possible? Or do you go, while you can, to learn a spell or charm that will let you do it faster? Do you make a bargain with a local god? Do you beat up the abysal who released the plague so that you can more effectively make a counteragent? Etc etc. This is micro level storytelling. This is focusing on the minutiae. We make the charms at this level because we assume that if the stories aren't interesting outside of micro level, we can abstract it to go more quickly, it's easier to cut details than add them.<br /> <br /> Now... in macro level setting resolution we say that this the minutiae of this action aren't important. roll for how many patients you save, use charms to say how many more you save. What matters is purely in the numbers. Don't have the charm that increases scale? Well, you weren't good enough this time. It certainly does a good job of illustrating that the character was amazing. But... we lost out on the individual stories. Which isn't to say that macro storytelling doesn't have it's place, just that it isn't the good default place. To say that Solars can overcome all limitations is great, but it's also great to take a moment to figure out HOW. Are there consequences? Because that might be interesting!<br /> <br /> It's not that solars can't interact with the setting at large, it's that skipping straight to that cuts out all the really interesting stories, and we wanna be able to tell them!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Stuff I've made:<br /> Ex3 Combat Flowchart &gt; <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/wnh21nkq07uuoct/Exalted%203rd%20Combat.png\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dropbox.com/s/wnh21nkq07...d%20Combat.png</a><br /> Ex3 Battlegroup Cheatsheet &gt; <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/byc19z0coe4kzhq/Exalted%203e%20Battlegroups.png?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dropbox.com/s/byc19z0coe...roups.png?dl=0</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9361-givenflesh","name":"GivenFlesh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9361_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:50:30-08:00","id":804108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ferryman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804097#post804097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think anyone doubts that a Solar, given the chance and the right skills, can chew through a thousand-man army. But he can only do that if the army cheerfully engages him on those terms. If his opponent instead splits his army into five 200-man raiding parties and starts burning down villages, though, it doesn't really <i>matter </i>that the Solar could kill all one thousand of them given the chance; the Solar can't protect his kingdom, and his people will lose faith in him because of that.<br /> <br /> That's the sort of thing that organizations and bureaucracies and communications and supply lines and, yes, having an army of your own are for. The writers have made it clear that they don't think Solars should be able to cheat those things away trivially, and should instead engage meaningfully with them. And that's great! So...can we have some material to help us do that?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure those things are very useful indeed in 3e, which is a marked improvement. Point is; Solars are not so restricted by scale as the OP is suggesting. Okay yeah technically your Solar Circle Sorcery can't just create random shit out of no-where, kind of, but you definitely can, by yourself, solve national scale problems through the application of spells, and you can do it at lower levels of sorcery through less potent spells and workings too. A Dawn can totally be the solution to &quot;there's an army attacking and we have a shitty little city guard&quot; problem; he <i>can</i> kill that army, and the threat of that means that you can engage in diplomacy with the line &quot;well, if you do try and pressure your claim against this tributary, I am sure that Gorebastard the Destroyer will take personal offence to that&quot; and it is a legitimate threat. One can imagine the rival prince looking at the gatehouse of his city, and the road to his citadel, and the ranks of his men, and thinking &quot;...yet all of that will not stop this one warrior if he decides to come for my head...&quot; and give up the claim. That's a thing! A cool thing! It's cool to be able to engage with stuff like that.<br /> <br /> Again (and I don't want to focus on one example) you can arbitrarily make it more difficult for one person to do these things in most examples, just like you can arbitrarily make it more difficult for a Solar to beat an adversary in an individual competition of excellence, and it's not a sure thing in either case in 3e, but the practical outcome of the rules in the book by players on a campaign setting include the overturning of scale problems, within reason, through individual capability.<br /> <br /> Which is cool. I like that in Exalted, you can do that stuff if you want to. 3e very much allows for it, too. Especially all the Sorcery!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 01-08-2016, 07:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T16:58:38-08:00","id":804112,"title":"","content":"Let me put it this way.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse goes out to strike deals with the harvest gods while the Twilight invents and implements agricultural advances across the villages and the Dawn goes to beat up the plague god that spreads a livestock disease, it's a story.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse pays ten motes for Famine Repelling Methodology, it's a joke.<br /> <br /> The former is the current state of affairs. The latter is what happens when you introduce a bureaucracy system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":13}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T17:02:32-08:00","id":804113,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804078#post804078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there's a point at which the rules have to be set aside a bit in portraying and approaching the setting; you can't really effectively use the battle group rules to portray armies of up to 30,000 troops or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not?<br /> <br /> It's 30 Size 5 battle groups, a few smaller battle groups of specialists, and a few named characters. You could go through them all in maybe ten fight scenes. The ST would have to do some work to keep it from becoming tedious, though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804078#post804078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a point where people start interacting with assumptions of things like geopolitics that are very similar to the real world. Even when that scale is so large that, in the time it takes you to get from one end of a full-scale military campaign to another, several cities have been devastated? What do you have in mind? That's part of the draw to some people, isn't it?</div> </div> </div> </div>To me, part of the draw is that it's geopolitics with vastly different assumptions from the ones in the real world.<br /> <br /> In real life, there aren't gods or shadowlands or one-man armies or any other supernatural forces. In Exalted, there are. Making real-world assumptions true in such a world would be both implausible and boring.<br /> <br /> As for the actual thread topic, I think John's talking nonsense. But I don't really have a problem with the design that resulted from that nonsense, and I don't think arguing it would be worthwhile, so I'm not going to go into detail.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T17:04:28-08:00","id":804115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804112#post804112\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me put it this way.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse goes out to strike deals with the harvest gods while the Twilight invents and implements agricultural advances across the villages and the Dawn goes to beat up the plague god that spreads a livestock disease, it's a story.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse pays ten motes for Famine Repelling Methodology, it's a joke.<br /> <br /> The former is the current state of affairs. The latter is what happens when you introduce a bureaucracy system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Weep for the un-included middle, comrades! I'm kidding, but I still think this is a simplification. A good bureaucracy system means that whole bit about implementing agricultural advances across the villages that the Twilight is doing is enhanced and made more enjoyable. A bad system does what you are suggesting, sure, but I don't think the system needs to be bad. It can be pretty good! The Sorcerous Workings system is a good example of a well written sub-system that enhances, rather than degrades, the games theme and the enjoyment of the players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T17:09:36-08:00","id":804120,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure if things top out exactly where Root suggests, necessarily (although I thought they were good examples, I don't have the system knowledge of 3e to say for sure).<br /> <br /> But I think they're more likely to top out in anyone's actual game more than they would if a totally abstract system was in place. Which is part of the point? Sort of restraining Solars kicking over the setting in an actively un-fun way by this being funneled through engaging with problems of how they bring their power to bear through the ordinary system. Rather than just like &quot;Oh I can do whatever because abstract resources and macro powers&quot;, thinking through in concrete terms how the Solar actually deals with the problems of scale. Which should be a more satisfying limit than some random numerical abstract numbers, because there is a concrete thought through logic to it? <br /> <br /> Although how big a deal people think those issues of scales actually are, probably will varies from group to group, as in this thread, with Solar on the &quot;they're relatively trivial; one man kill-factory is fine&quot; end and others on the &quot;huge impediment&quot; end. I'm not so sure there's a systematically right answer. (Some of this may vary on how much your taste is more &quot;Dynasty Warriors one man army, yay&quot; or &quot;Er... don't care about that. I sort of got into this to play a character who's as smart as Zhuge Liang actually is at war strategy, and fights like Li Mu Bai, without that scaling up to, him personally fighting a massive army.&quot;?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T17:30:16-08:00","id":804137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804115#post804115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Weep for the un-included middle, comrades! I'm kidding, but I still think this is a simplification. A good bureaucracy system means that whole bit about implementing agricultural advances across the villages that the Twilight is doing is enhanced and made more enjoyable. A bad system does what you are suggesting, sure, but I don't think the system needs to be bad. It can be pretty good! The Sorcerous Workings system is a good example of a well written sub-system that enhances, rather than degrades, the games theme and the enjoyment of the players.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> In the Exalted forum there are no middle grounds or concepts of irony.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Exalted: Princes of the Universe<br /> A Third Edition Exalted Podcast<br /> <b><u><a href=\"http://atrusilk.podbean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://atrusilk.podbean.com/</a></u></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8769-tavelgorge","name":"TavelGorge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"498"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T17:56:50-08:00","id":804154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804112#post804112\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me put it this way.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse goes out to strike deals with the harvest gods while the Twilight invents and implements agricultural advances across the villages and the Dawn goes to beat up the plague god that spreads a livestock disease, it's a story.<br /> <br /> If a famine strikes the kingdom, and the Eclipse pays ten motes for Famine Repelling Methodology, it's a joke.<br /> <br /> The former is the current state of affairs. The latter is what happens when you introduce a bureaucracy system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely. I for one am quite happy with the way 3rd ed does this level of things.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I'm a little confused by many of the assumptions on this thread; I find geopolitics both reasonably easy and quite fun to run, without needing much of a system for the macro-level (I never used any of the Mandate of Creation stuff).<br /> The real issue is time; I'm impatient for the players' actions to have effects on a regional scale, when of course, should take months to filter through.<br /> The main trick is not overwhelming the players with problems on a regional scale, let the information come reasonably slowly, and give them plenty of downtime.<br /> <br /> <br /> I should also say that, due to the themes and nature of the the game, monarchy-style rulership is the most likely way for PCs to interact with geopolitics. And monarchy is an extremely personal-scale way of doing politics: wars are fought because the princess married this guy and not that guy; when there's a famine or plague the King goes to beg the gods to save the country, and if he doesn't beg well enough or the gods are feeling dickish, he'll likely be deposed; control is maintained by the King literally partying his way across the country, personally meeting and greeting different nobles and charming (or threatening) them.<br /> <br /> So, running the country is a story, interacting with other countries is a story. And when the PCs come up with a plan for economic rejuvanation, we roleplay through it, (with a few Bureaucracy rolls of course; Int+Bureacracy to notice problems they haven't thought about so they don't do a Mao, Charisma+Bureaucracy to order around bureaucrats, etc). And that's fine.<br /> <br /> The thing about a bureaucracy or ruling a country system, is that it gives mechanical hooks for bureaucracy charms to hang off. But I think the 3rd ed Bureaucracy charms are pretty good (the 2nd ed ones were not so good), so that's fine too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 01-08-2016, 09:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T23:34:54-08:00","id":804277,"title":"","content":"Interesting post Root.<br /> <br /> Re: fighting a plague: you will find that a Solar with Medicine alone will have a problem, but one with Investigation, Lore, Survival, and Craft can do it.<br /> <br /> Investigation and Lore helps the Solar figure out the source of the plague. Survival can help them confirm it in the case of getting close enough to carrier animals. She could be led to discover the source lies in bush meat trade and even isolate the monkeys causing it. In some cases, bad water supply is to blame, and Craft can help her engineer solutions. Medicine helps her figure out what kind of solutions are plausible. Lore can also help if historical records reveal any sort of effective response used in the past.<br /> <br /> Bureaucracy can also help in creating market models that espouse safe practices, while Lore can be used to educate people on things like hygiene and how to avoid spreading disease when hygiene won't cut it. <br /> <br /> There isn't a Charm to just end a massive plague outbreak, but with some elbow grease and time the Lawgiver can fight a fire that it takes a modern national relief effort to quell. All of these things require time, and an emphasis on roleplaying and storytelling.<br /> <br /> We decided to try to avoid ever writing Charms that resolve conflicts, because those conflicts are where the game lives. It is impossible to feel like you are playing a Solar if you are never challenged to use your Abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":16}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/86-john-m\u00f8rke","name":"John M\u00f8rke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar86_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T23:43:09-08:00","id":804278,"title":"","content":"Mantra of the Solar Hospitaler:<br /> <br /> If you want to know the illness, which medicine to use, and the dose: Medicine<br /> If you want to know which plant or animal parts are used to make the medicine and where to find them: Lore<br /> If you want to avoid dying in the jungle or touching dangerous plants while harvesting medicinal herbs: Survival<br /> If you want to synthesize medicines from harvested herbs: Craft<br /> If you want to figure out how people are getting infected: Investigation<br /> If you suspect a plague spirit might be behind it: Occult<br /> If you think market practices are to blame: Bureaucracy","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":17}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/86-john-m\u00f8rke","name":"John M\u00f8rke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar86_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T23:44:30-08:00","id":804279,"title":"","content":"If you think you might have trouble fighting an outbreak in a foreign land: Lore, Linguistics, Socialize.<br /> <br /> Also known as your friend, the Eclipse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":14}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/86-john-m\u00f8rke","name":"John M\u00f8rke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar86_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T00:52:56-08:00","id":804290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804277#post804277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting post Root.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you wrote half of it, so my job was pretty easy. But I think it's important to know where Charms are supposed to stop and where plans take over, so I'm glad we're all thinking more about that.<br /> <br /> Since the question of how we come up with good challenges for leadership projects has come up a few times, will <i>The Realm</i> end up being a good source for folks who are trying to get a better handle on that?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":2010,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","user":"Root","date":". 01-09-2016, 05:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T04:56:30-08:00","id":804370,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804115#post804115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Weep for the un-included middle, comrades! I'm kidding, but I still think this is a simplification. A good bureaucracy system means that whole bit about implementing agricultural advances across the villages that the Twilight is doing is enhanced and made more enjoyable. A bad system does what you are suggesting, sure, but I don't think the system needs to be bad. It can be pretty good! The Sorcerous Workings system is a good example of a well written sub-system that enhances, rather than degrades, the games theme and the enjoyment of the players.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorcerous Workings need a subsystem because they have no real life equivalent to base results on - IRL, the most you can do with being a sorcerer is scam people for money, or maybe get laid. We need rules to establish that Workings are a thing at all, the scope of what they can achieve, how hard it is, and what happens when the sorcerer fails. What makes them fun is that they exist at all, that the player of a sorcerer gets a license to do things beyond the scope of casting fireballs and summoning demonic slaves and such. The rules attached to it are a necessity, they don't enhance the game just by virtue of being a couple rules requiring a couple rolls.<br /> <br /> This middle ground I've excluded... it exists, theoretically, but I've yet to see a single bureaucracy/kingdom ruling subsystem that meaningfully taps into it, so forgive me for not paying it much heed. Such systems abstract and simplify things to a large degree by their very nature, and the end result is that you start thinking of things in terms of stats and modifiers rather than the actual problem at hand and how your character can contribute his singular skills to the task.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T05:01:37-08:00","id":804374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804279#post804279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you think you might have trouble fighting an outbreak in a foreign land: Lore, Linguistics, Socialize.<br /> <br /> Also known as your friend, the Eclipse.</div> </div> </div> </div>By the same standard, you can be the Eclipse and hire or charm a Twilight to be your nerdy sidekick while you are the genuis doctor and take all the glory, does that count <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3332,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3332-4uk4ata","user":"4uk4ata","date":". 01-09-2016, 08:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3332-4uk4ata","name":"4uk4ata","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T08:03:02-08:00","id":804457,"title":"","content":"I'd like more support for supernatural complications, at least somewhere down the line.<br /> <br /> I can basically go basically anywhere that discusses rpgs or creative writing and ask &quot;so these characters are building a bridge what can realistically go wrong with that and how can I make it interesting?&quot; but how likely it is for gods to involve themselves is something that the Exalted fanbase has little consensus on and overusing &quot;the spirits consider this an affront&quot; runs the risk of the players blaming the gods for the setting's technological stasis or thinking that the Immaculate Order has the right idea.<br /> <br /> Similarly, having the antagonist factions constantly trying to wreck your shit is very Saturday morning cartoon villainy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T08:19:40-08:00","id":804465,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804457#post804457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like more support for supernatural complications, at least somewhere down the line.<br /> <br /> I can basically go basically anywhere that discusses rpgs or creative writing and ask &quot;so these characters are building a bridge what can realistically go wrong with that and how can I make it interesting?&quot; but how likely it is for gods to involve themselves is something that the Exalted fanbase has little consensus on and overusing &quot;the spirits consider this an affront&quot; runs the risk of the players blaming the gods for the setting's technological stasis or thinking that the Immaculate Order has the right idea.<br /> <br /> Similarly, having the antagonist factions constantly trying to wreck your shit is very Saturday morning cartoon villainy.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have seen a lot of call for a book that opens up the setting for &quot;hot use&quot; by teaching players how things work, aside from just telling them to go read things.<br /> <br /> I haven't figured out exactly how to do it yet, but I am on the case. For now, I highly suggest devouring the books in the suggested reading list at the front of the core.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":13}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/86-john-m\u00f8rke","name":"John M\u00f8rke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar86_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T09:21:38-08:00","id":804498,"title":"","content":"This is a really great discussion Root! Keeping charms as part of the plan instead of the plan itself is good writing.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804465#post804465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have seen a lot of call for a book that opens up the setting for &quot;hot use&quot; by teaching players how things work, aside from just telling them to go read things.<br /> <br /> I haven't figured out exactly how to do it yet, but I am on the case. For now, I highly suggest devouring the books in the suggested reading list at the front of the core.</div> </div> </div> </div>Great to hear, John! You guys have been busy writing a lot of great books <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.<br /> Maybe you could come up with a &quot;challenges of the Exalted&quot; type book? Situations where the players have had time to understand their abilities and/or grow in power, and the old challenges seem insufficient (or at least need to be executed differently?)<br /> For example, &quot;these are the basic challenges, or some sample challenges a brand new group of Solars/Dragonblooded/Exigents face, then here's what's likely to go wrong as they grow in power (craft new artifacts or projects, raise and train their armies or businesses, create a sorcerous working). And finally, here are the types of challenges a pack of Essence 5s is likely face.<br /> <br /> Or, if you want to do it from a player's perspective, here are some challenges your powers are likely to <i>fix</i> that you may not have considered.<br /> <br /> Does that help? I'm sure you've dealt with at least some of that in the new core (haven't seen the book yet <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) but wondering how to handle a group's growing power is something I struggle with when I ST, and it seems to be a problems for a lot of other GMs as well (including many I've seen outside of Exalted).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T10:30:30-08:00","id":804532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804465#post804465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have seen a lot of call for a book that opens up the setting for &quot;hot use&quot; by teaching players how things work, aside from just telling them to go read things.<br /> <br /> I haven't figured out exactly how to do it yet, but I am on the case. For now, I highly suggest devouring the books in the suggested reading list at the front of the core.</div> </div> </div> </div>While this is indeed a great suggestion, <i>what am I gonna do about these papercuts on my tongue?!?</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T10:52:36-08:00","id":804546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804532#post804532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While this is indeed a great suggestion, <i>what am I gonna do about these papercuts on my tongue?!?</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Hint: dip the paper in a fluid of your choice before eating it - coffee lets you devour more books faster, but I heard dipping them in the spinal fluid of a certified genius helps with fact retention.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T11:34:43-08:00","id":804575,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804498#post804498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a really great discussion Root! Keeping charms as part of the plan instead of the plan itself is good writing.<br /> <br /> <br /> Great to hear, John! You guys have been busy writing a lot of great books <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.<br /> Maybe you could come up with a &quot;challenges of the Exalted&quot; type book? Situations where the players have had time to understand their abilities and/or grow in power, and the old challenges seem insufficient (or at least need to be executed differently?)<br /> For example, &quot;these are the basic challenges, or some sample challenges a brand new group of Solars/Dragonblooded/Exigents face, then here's what's likely to go wrong as they grow in power (craft new artifacts or projects, raise and train their armies or businesses, create a sorcerous working). And finally, here are the types of challenges a pack of Essence 5s is likely face.<br /> <br /> Or, if you want to do it from a player's perspective, here are some challenges your powers are likely to <i>fix</i> that you may not have considered.<br /> <br /> Does that help? I'm sure you've dealt with at least some of that in the new core (haven't seen the book yet <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) but wondering how to handle a group's growing power is something I struggle with when I ST, and it seems to be a problems for a lot of other GMs as well (including many I've seen outside of Exalted).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> One of the big things we want to do with supplemental setting material is to make the material seem alive and current. The mood should be the farthest thing from reading like a scroll you dusted off, a concrete history that is dead the moment you lay hands on it. <br /> <br /> One of our ideas for handling that is to link setting material with dice rolls. For example, &quot;This is the Lore difficulty to introduce a fact about [First Age secret you just read] and this is the Lore background you need to even recognize this material in play&quot; and &quot;If you roll [this many] Lore successes and have the appropriate background, you can introduce one of the following facts:[list of details about a lost culture being discussed].&quot;<br /> <br /> IE, we have recognized a link between higher levels of interactivity with the setting material and the perception that the setting feels alive, and thus setting books are necessary.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":16}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/86-john-m\u00f8rke","name":"John M\u00f8rke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar86_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T11:46:11-08:00","id":804581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804575#post804575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> One of our ideas for handling that is to link setting material with dice rolls. For example, &quot;This is the Lore difficulty to introduce a fact about [First Age secret you just read] and this is the Lore background you need to even recognize this material in play&quot; and &quot;If you roll [this many] Lore successes and have the appropriate background, you can introduce one of the following facts: [list of details about a lost culture being discussed].&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I approve of this initiative.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T11:57:54-08:00","id":804595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why not? It's 30 Size 5 battle groups... The ST would have to do some work to keep it from becoming tedious, though.</div> </div> </div> </div> I believe that question answers itself. I mean, thirty size 5 battle groups? In a system that addresses the issue of the mechanical and gameplay strains of fighting thirty people by turning them into a battle group? <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could go through them all in maybe ten fight scenes.</div> </div> </div> </div> Why does this army not move? <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In real life, there aren't gods or shadowlands or one-man armies or any other supernatural forces. In Exalted, there are. Making real-world assumptions true in such a world would be both implausible and boring.</div> </div> </div> </div> It's not quite about the assumptions being the same. More about the assumptions creating a simplified world which doesn't really resonate with the complexity that is in everything ever. I adore the map we've been given of the setting, but I would be unsatisfied with the idea of having to play inside of that rather than the territory. That and just how some things interact; that city whose entire defense plan revolves around a single individual is going to be in dire straights when the Solar has to deal with something such as an army that splits into two groups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T12:26:52-08:00","id":804614,"title":"","content":"Really if you want to get into the detail of a scenario where a Dawn, or indeed any Solar, can personally fight an army made up several large battlegroups of various stripes, then we can, but it feels a little like you are trying to get people to painstakingly explain exactly how it's possible within the rules with an eye towards changing the scenario in any given instance to invalidate that. It's possible, and if you read the charms and play the game, that's not an aberration, it's pretty natural. I've done it! The army can move around and attack you and stuff, but once you get into them then it doesn't take many combat rounds for several thousand troops to be killed or driven off. You get into melee range, and start making attacks, enhanced with charms. Their magnitude drops, and the can't effectively hurt you back because you use defensive charms and such. Or you do the same with archery charms or whatever. Pew pew! It's very cool!<br /> <br /> And it's not really going to be in dire straights if that army splits into two groups, because if you know that the enemy army has split into two groups, a day's march away, in different places, then a Solar can go to one, kill them, then go to the other and kill them before they get to the city using all manner of options (athletics charms! sorcery! artifacts!). And this is without getting into the various wranglings this army's prince is going to have to do to persuade his various lordlings to come and take part in this military escapade. &quot;Gorebastard can only kill one army at a time, and we have two armies! So the chances of you dying are only 50/50!&quot; &quot;But if the other army gets to the city and conquers it, won't Gorebastard be really angry and then come and kill the rest of us as well?&quot; &quot;Errrr...&quot;<br /> <br /> Again, if you <i>arbitrarily</i> add in additional caveats which are deliberately based on making it harder for the PC in this scenario it does cause them problems, but that's not the point, the point is that the power of the Solars does go deep into scale as is obviously the case. A single man who can fight and kill an entire army by himself is a <i>big deal</i> and should be treated as such! Solars can do that, so why pretend that this isn't something that would have a big impact on a national level?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 01-09-2016, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T12:56:19-08:00","id":804642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804595#post804595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I believe that question answers itself. I mean, thirty size 5 battle groups? In a system that addresses the issue of the mechanical and gameplay strains of fighting thirty people by turning them into a battle group?</div> </div> </div> </div>A battle group is basically just an NPC, and most games will have at least 30 NPCs.<br /> <br /> Yes, 30 battle groups is too many for a single fight. But it's not too many for a story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804595#post804595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why does this army not move?</div> </div> </div> </div>It does, of course.<br /> <br /> One way I'd try to make this story interesting, as a ST, is to introduce some chases. The Solars would be guaranteed to catch the armies eventually, since small groups move much faster than large ones, but maybe eventually wouldn't be good enough.<br /> <br /> I doubt the enemy army would be dumb enough to pillage the Solars' city, if they have one, while the Solars are away. Enraging someone you're too scared to fight is just ridiculously dumb for a group too large to run and hide. But there are other things they could do.<br /> <br /> And then of course there are the inevitable collapses of morale on the other side. Surrendering enemies can be more problematic than ones that want to fight to the death, if you're a nigh-invincible super-warrior.<br /> <br /> If all else fails, I can declare that the one-sided massacres led to the formation of Shadowlands.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T14:16:29-08:00","id":804693,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804277#post804277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting post Root.<br /> <br /> Re: fighting a plague: you will find that a Solar with Medicine alone will have a problem, but one with Investigation, Lore, Survival, and Craft can do it.<br /> <br /> Investigation and Lore helps the Solar figure out the source of the plague. Survival can help them confirm it in the case of getting close enough to carrier animals. She could be led to discover the source lies in bush meat trade and even isolate the monkeys causing it. In some cases, bad water supply is to blame, and Craft can help her engineer solutions. Medicine helps her figure out what kind of solutions are plausible. Lore can also help if historical records reveal any sort of effective response used in the past.<br /> <br /> Bureaucracy can also help in creating market models that espouse safe practices, while Lore can be used to educate people on things like hygiene and how to avoid spreading disease when hygiene won't cut it.<br /> <br /> There isn't a Charm to just end a massive plague outbreak, but with some elbow grease and time the Lawgiver can fight a fire that it takes a modern national relief effort to quell. All of these things require time, and an emphasis on roleplaying and storytelling.<br /> <br /> We decided to try to avoid ever writing Charms that resolve conflicts, because those conflicts are where the game lives. It is impossible to feel like you are playing a Solar if you are never challenged to use your Abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like a sidebar or two on this sort of thing would have been nice. Something about designing adventures for Solars to pull all these things together like that.<br /> <br /> I'm also not sure that a Solar medic can keep a hospice full of people with the plague alive by themselves. I mean, yes, stopping the source of the plague is very important in preventing new cases, but what do you do about all the existing sick people?<br /> <br /> Edit: Good to hear that you've got an eye towards doing this sort of thing John.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":226,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","user":"BrilliantRain","date":". 01-09-2016, 05:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T14:28:12-08:00","id":804705,"title":"","content":"John, while you're here, I have a question about the Solar Bureaucracy charms. I'm quite happy about not having macro scale charms and charms that don't instantly resolve conflicts...but it feels like there are several Bureaucracy charms that are &quot;Snap your fingers and organization gets fixed&quot; or &quot;I put an abstract blessing on this group,&quot; instead of &quot;Because I'm just really good at running an organization my people work better.&quot; So, I guess I'd like some clarity on how things like Bureau-Reforming Kata and Indolent Official Charm are supposed to work. Are they really just magic spells you put on an organization?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T17:33:21-08:00","id":804843,"title":"","content":"I think arguing logistics of how many Size 5 battlegroups a single Solar can take on and whether he can keep pace with multiple armies coming from different directions misses the single crucial point: why would he do that? Does he really think this is the best way to use his considerable martial talent, to just run around his domain and slaughter any and every threat personally? More importantly, how is it more effective this way than with a solid army at his back, dealing with threats that don't require his direct input and holding ground to buy him time when his presence is actually required?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T18:13:40-08:00","id":804873,"title":"","content":"Obviously the Solar is better off raising an army of their own. And negotiating a peace agreement is even better than that.<br /> <br /> I'm just trying to say, you can actually play out &quot;PCs vs 30 000&quot; if you want to. And you might want to...maybe a heroic Circle wants to go save some faraway country from a huge army of zombies led by an Abyssal, and the only way to get there fast enough is Stormwind Rider. Can't carry an army with that.<br /> <br /> It's not something that'll happen often, obviously, but the game system can handle it if it does.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T18:58:50-08:00","id":804912,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804873#post804873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Obviously the Solar is better off raising an army of their own. And negotiating a peace agreement is even better than that.<br /> <br /> I'm just trying to say, you can actually play out &quot;PCs vs 30 000&quot; if you want to. And you might want to...maybe a heroic Circle wants to go save some faraway country from a huge army of zombies led by an Abyssal, and the only way to get there fast enough is Stormwind Rider. Can't carry an army with that.<br /> <br /> It's not something that'll happen often, obviously, but the game system can handle it if it does.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not disputing that. This may come up, the PCs may pull through such a scenario without relying on extra forces, and it may make for an awesome session or two if handled well.<br /> <br /> What I'm disputing, and what I think Isator was disputing, is the idea that any sane Solar king would consider this scenario his baseline policy for dealing with external threats to his rule.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T21:59:19-08:00","id":805011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804498#post804498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a really great discussion Root! Keeping charms as part of the plan instead of the plan itself is good writing.<br /> <br /> <br /> Great to hear, John! You guys have been busy writing a lot of great books <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.<br /> Maybe you could come up with a &quot;challenges of the Exalted&quot; type book? Situations where the players have had time to understand their abilities and/or grow in power, and the old challenges seem insufficient (or at least need to be executed differently?)<br /> For example, &quot;these are the basic challenges, or some sample challenges a brand new group of Solars/Dragonblooded/Exigents face, then here's what's likely to go wrong as they grow in power (craft new artifacts or projects, raise and train their armies or businesses, create a sorcerous working). And finally, here are the types of challenges a pack of Essence 5s is likely face.<br /> <br /> Or, if you want to do it from a player's perspective, here are some challenges your powers are likely to <i>fix</i> that you may not have considered.<br /> <br /> Does that help? I'm sure you've dealt with at least some of that in the new core (haven't seen the book yet <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) but wondering how to handle a group's growing power is something I struggle with when I ST, and it seems to be a problems for a lot of other GMs as well (including many I've seen outside of Exalted).</div> </div> </div> </div>Possibly an adventure or adventure sequence along with notes on how players might tackle the problems? If written properly, it might help out with a lot of this stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-09T22:05:42-08:00","id":805012,"title":"","content":"Also, having finally caught up with the thread, one of my take away points is that the Solar Medic needing to work herself to the bone trying to keep as many plague victims alive, while the rest of the party hunts down the source of the disease and fixes it, is considered a feature, not a bug. Which, yeah...That does seem like a better state of affairs, <br /> <br /> Though, now I want to try putting, say, 50 plague victims in a room and just see how many of them a maxed out Twilight Medicine Supernal Doctor can cure, just to see what the high water benchmarks should be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T17:11:47-08:00","id":805620,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804912#post804912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not disputing that. This may come up, the PCs may pull through such a scenario without relying on extra forces, and it may make for an awesome session or two if handled well.<br /> <br /> What I'm disputing, and what I think Isator was disputing, is the idea that any sane Solar king would consider this scenario his baseline policy for dealing with external threats to his rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>But what sane soldier would follow a plan to sacrifice themself and half their army while the other half raids a city then gets massacred shortly after for no real gain?<br /> <br /> Also it's a bit unfair to present a scenario then say it doesn't make sense when people try to argue it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/QIvvuAN.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9998-puppyblew","name":"PuppyBlew","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9998_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T17:30:15-08:00","id":805634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804912#post804912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not disputing that. This may come up, the PCs may pull through such a scenario without relying on extra forces, and it may make for an awesome session or two if handled well.<br /> <br /> What I'm disputing, and what I think Isator was disputing, is the idea that any sane Solar king would consider this scenario his baseline policy for dealing with external threats to his rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not? It would work, maybe not as effectively as some other options, but it'd work and potentially those options are not available. Armies are not cheap. Manpower isn't unlimited. And sending out your own 1000 troops to fight and die when you don't need them and can defeat the enemy army by yourself could even be something that a virtuous warrior sees as immoral (none of my people need die while I protect them!)<br /> <br /> Let's say you're the ruler of a half dozen villages around a small town that a thousand men could bring to heel. Now let's say that a Solar has shacked up in the town, for whatever reason, and that Solar is a White Reaper style martial artist, or has a bunch of Melee charms, or is a sorcerer etc etc etc. Now those thousand troops are in trouble, never mind the locals, that Solar is a threat to them by themselves! Suddenly this minor little province is a much trickier prospect. That's what I mean by saying that Solars can overcome elements of scale; simply by the nature of how individually powerful they are, they overcome certain elements of scale.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T17:35:17-08:00","id":805638,"title":"","content":"I don't think there's any disputing that Solars can do a lot. What doesn't make much sense though is for them to not be human, that is, to rule over a society or run an organization <i>for which they have no need.</i> The Solar could fight his kingdom's enemies alone if needed, but why do it? How does he even know where his enemies are, how strong they are, or what they've planned? Even if he destroys armies, what does he do against small, numerous, more mobile groups of raiders and saboteurs? There's a reason superheroes in the comics don't dissolve the city's police force and just use their superior capabilities to tackle all crime alone. Sure, Batman can solve any given crime better than the police, but it's absurd if he solves all of them alone. It's impossible to be everywhere and know everything, and wanting to do so is pretty bizarre (not in the &quot;I wish I could do more,&quot; sense, but in the, &quot;I actually want to be an entire government/army/industry,&quot; one), so why not let other people handle what they can?<br /> <br /> Just as we don't have charms for the Solar to know where every criminal or bandit or spy in her empire is, so we also don't have - and shouldn't have - charms to let the Solar replace all the craftsmen, ministers, farmers, and other workers and experts she ought to rely on at times. The Solar rules her lands, but she isn't the only one with an important job or meaningful agency.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T17:39:25-08:00","id":805639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>John M\u00f8rke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=804575#post804575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> One of the big things we want to do with supplemental setting material is to make the material seem alive and current. The mood should be the farthest thing from reading like a scroll you dusted off, a concrete history that is dead the moment you lay hands on it.<br /> <br /> One of our ideas for handling that is to link setting material with dice rolls. For example, &quot;This is the Lore difficulty to introduce a fact about [First Age secret you just read] and this is the Lore background you need to even recognize this material in play&quot; and &quot;If you roll [this many] Lore successes and have the appropriate background, you can introduce one of the following facts: [list of details about a lost culture being discussed].&quot;<br /> <br /> IE, we have recognized a link between higher levels of interactivity with the setting material and the perception that the setting feels alive, and thus setting books are necessary.<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry to reply to this a page later... but that's an excellent idea and you should definitely do that, because people constantly wonder exactly what their characters know and don't know about the setting. Which historical, political, and magical details are common knowledge and which are not? So this will tell them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T17:51:16-08:00","id":805646,"title":"","content":"Even apart from the fact that there's a scope at which the resolution of something like battle group mechanics becoming fuzzy or just plain not returning coherent results, for me it's mostly a matter of priorities. I <i>want</i> to frame the issues of large scale warfare in terms other than &quot;you fight a few dozen regular guys with some +5s to their attack stats with your gaming group sized Exalted attack force&quot; so that things like the Realm having armies makes sense. And I <i>want</i> the Realm having armies to make sense, because stories of the scope and organization of war are very interesting to me.<br /> <br /> Plus, there's a point at which one person or five people fighting tens of thousands just looks silly rather than impressive to me; the bad fight choreography of &quot;the ninjas surround the hero and then politely go in one at a time and roll out of the way after a single punch&quot; taking to a ludicrous scale, a scale where you've got some of those ninjas <i>miles</i> away from the individual they're standing in line to be punched down by*.<br /> <br /> Hence, when the story has to go off into territory that the system has not mapped, and I have to describe what is found there, I will describe something that fulfills those priorities.<br /> <br /> * That image of them waiting in a line that stretches over miles makes me think of another historical point, referring back to that point of why the army is not moving.<br /> <br /> Because of course, historically most armies have not stayed around to actually be slaughtered once it's apparent that they have lost. You have an army of 30,000, you don't need to lose much more than 2,000 before seeing that it's time to pack it in, preferably in a direction away from that line consisting of thousands of spears and horses.<br /> <br /> It's a matter of picturing basic geometry to see how that army that is not going to stay past the first few thousand casualties can go in a greater range of directions, because no matter how dangerous he is, one person is not quite the obstacle to a group spread over several miles that an army is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T18:25:06-08:00","id":805664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805646#post805646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even apart from the fact that there's a scope at which the resolution of something like battle group mechanics becoming fuzzy or just plain not returning coherent results, for me it's mostly a matter of priorities. I <i>want</i> to frame the issues of large scale warfare in terms other than &amp;quot;you fight a few dozen regular guys with some +5s to their attack stats with your gaming group sized Exalted attack force&amp;quot; so that things like the Realm having armies makes sense. And I <i>want</i> the Realm having armies to make sense, because stories of the scope and organization of war are very interesting to me.<br /> <br /> Plus, there's a point at which one person or five people fighting tens of thousands just looks silly rather than impressive to me; the bad fight choreography of &amp;quot;the ninjas surround the hero and then politely go in one at a time and roll out of the way after a single punch&amp;quot; taking to a ludicrous scale, a scale where you've got some of those ninjas <i>miles</i> away from the individual they're standing in line to be punched down by*.<br /> <br /> Hence, when the story has to go off into territory that the system has not mapped, and I have to describe what is found there, I will describe something that fulfills those priorities.<br /> <br /> * That image of them waiting in a line that stretches over miles makes me think of another historical point, referring back to that point of why the army is not moving.<br /> <br /> Because of course, historically most armies have not stayed around to actually be slaughtered once it's apparent that they have lost. You have an army of 30,000, you don't need to lose much more than 2,000 before seeing that it's time to pack it in, preferably in a direction away from that line consisting of thousands of spears and horses.<br /> <br /> It's a matter of picturing basic geometry to see how that army that is not going to stay past the first few thousand casualties can go in a greater range of directions, because no matter how dangerous he is, one person is not quite the obstacle to a group spread over several miles that an army is.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see how a sufficiently skilled Solar being able to take down an army makes the Realm having armies not make sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/QIvvuAN.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9998-puppyblew","name":"PuppyBlew","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9998_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T18:27:06-08:00","id":805666,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805646#post805646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even apart from the fact that there's a scope at which the resolution of something like battle group mechanics becoming fuzzy or just plain not returning coherent results, for me it's mostly a matter of priorities. I <i>want</i> to frame the issues of large scale warfare in terms other than &amp;quot;you fight a few dozen regular guys with some +5s to their attack stats with your gaming group sized Exalted attack force&amp;quot; so that things like the Realm having armies makes sense. And I <i>want</i> the Realm having armies to make sense, because stories of the scope and organization of war are very interesting to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah pretty much. In another example a Solar with supernal survival should be able to find food for him and his allies even in the mids of a desolate wasteland. Saying that a Solar can personally provide for a city of 100,000 out in the middle of a dry sea of sand by doing nothing more than applying his inhuman wilderness survival ability makes for an uninteresting circumstance.<br /> <br /> One solar should be able to make a huge impact on desert city land, but not by personally using his charms to solve the whole problem. Likewise an entire invading army shouldn't be a problem solvable by 4 melee charms. Exalted is pretty sweet in that it lets you fight 30 random dudes at ones and win pretty easily, but the system breaks down at scales of tens of thousands I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T18:28:13-08:00","id":805667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PuppyBlew</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805664#post805664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't see how a sufficiently skilled Solar being able to take down an army makes the Realm having armies not make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Keeping in mind that with the current system it's not terribly difficult for a sufficiently skilled DB to kill an army of 10,000 all on his own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-10T19:14:41-08:00","id":805697,"title":"","content":"A Solar fighting an army can at least be made to make sense and require some thought. Maybe she positions herself at a bridge or ford or narrow pass. She uses her abilities and charms to hold her position and prevent enemy troops from passing her without being scattered or killed. She's risking her well-being and diverting her effort from the real tasks of leading.<br /> <br /> What makes almost no sense to me, if we're interested in the challenges of leadership, is having the Solar roll Bureaucracy to pull spare peasants out of the woodwork, War to make them into perfect soldiers, and then Craft to kit them out in the finest arms and armor. And then she repeats this trick in the next place the next day or week, as long as she feels like it.<br /> <br /> That's why I like that finding people and getting them to do what you want has more to do with story considerations and common sense than dice rolls. Tiger Warriors are a thing, but the Solar can't afford to keep more than a unit or two of such elite warriors around. And of course the scope of Craft is such that the Solar is much better off letting other people make things than trying to CNNT her way to 1,000 shields, spears, and breastplates. Even sorcery can't just shrug off all these limitations on one person's effort and grasp.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T01:10:04-08:00","id":805887,"title":"","content":"One of things for me about a Solar who is an army killer is that, although the choreography is not easy to imagine (there aren't many references), when I can imagine that, personally murdering 100's-1000's of people, with all the flesh slicing and arrows to the throat that implies, and not the sanitised Dynasty Warriors / shmup version, is actually a pretty horrific scene, and would be for a character who wasn't a bit of a psychopath (the closest parallels in cinema and literature are using limit breaking in a way - The Bride, Elric, etc, and even then on a lesser scale). I guess there might be a lot of drama inherent in that though....?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T01:42:59-08:00","id":805901,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805646#post805646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even apart from the fact that there's a scope at which the resolution of something like battle group mechanics becoming fuzzy or just plain not returning coherent results, for me it's mostly a matter of priorities. I <i>want</i> to frame the issues of large scale warfare in terms other than &quot;you fight a few dozen regular guys with some +5s to their attack stats with your gaming group sized Exalted attack force&quot; so that things like the Realm having armies makes sense. And I <i>want</i> the Realm having armies to make sense, because stories of the scope and organization of war are very interesting to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even by strict RAW, having an army makes loads of sense. Battle groups are pretty potent if they're competently led.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805646#post805646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, there's a point at which one person or five people fighting tens of thousands just looks silly rather than impressive to me; the bad fight choreography of &quot;the ninjas surround the hero and then politely go in one at a time and roll out of the way after a single punch&quot; taking to a ludicrous scale, a scale where you've got some of those ninjas <i>miles</i> away from the individual they're standing in line to be punched down by*.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> Because of course, historically most armies have not stayed around to actually be slaughtered once it's apparent that they have lost. You have an army of 30,000, you don't need to lose much more than 2,000 before seeing that it's time to pack it in, preferably in a direction away from that line consisting of thousands of spears and horses.<br /> <br /> It's a matter of picturing basic geometry to see how that army that is not going to stay past the first few thousand casualties can go in a greater range of directions, because no matter how dangerous he is, one person is not quite the obstacle to a group spread over several miles that an army is.</div> </div> </div> </div>One problem solves the other, I think. A horde of 1000 charges you, you kill 50 in short order, hundreds realize they're not being paid enough for this and flee. The battle group loses a point of Size. The next horde of 1000 says, &quot;what a bunch of cowards&quot; and charges.<br /> <br /> If the first 1000 was wiped out to the last man, it'd make the second 1000 look like fools...but chances are, the losses are 90% morale-related. Fortunately soldiers who've fled are only very slightly less beaten than ones who've died.<br /> <br /> The exception being units with Perfect Morale, like zombies. But it's perfectly acceptable for 1000 zombies to walk into a sword.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Root</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805697#post805697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's why I like that finding people and getting them to do what you want has more to do with story considerations and common sense than dice rolls. Tiger Warriors are a thing, but the Solar can't afford to keep more than a unit or two of such elite warriors around. And of course the scope of Craft is such that the Solar is much better off letting other people make things than trying to CNNT her way to 1,000 shields, spears, and breastplates. Even sorcery can't just shrug off all these limitations on one person's effort and grasp.</div> </div> </div> </div>By the book, you can train tens of thousands of Tiger Warriors...if you spend years doing it. Which I like.<br /> <br /> I don't have a problem with building cities single-handedly or personally re-writing the weather of an entire country with a sorcerous working or whatever. It just shouldn't be too quick.<br /> <br /> Exactly how quick is too quick is an open question. My gut instinct is that a Solar alone can be about as fast as 20 mortal masters, and that they can get things done much much faster than that when inspiring and leading a large group.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T01:53:51-08:00","id":805904,"title":"","content":"Hmmm, poosssibly. After all, another implication of the fact that armies don't wait around to be killed to the last soldier is that through the majority of history, the majority of soldiers never actually kill anybody. Even when they do, part of the point of army structures is to spread it out and render it kind of impersonal; it can often be hard to be sure that you, personally, have killed somebody, even before the advent of firearms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T02:43:17-08:00","id":805928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805901#post805901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the book, you can train tens of thousands of Tiger Warriors...if you spend years doing it. Which I like.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure. The duration is Indefinite, and there is no clause ending the mote commitment and making the effects permanent as there is for League of Iron Preparation. That seems to suggest that the Tiger Warriors are the Exalt's personal army, not a group of people she trained for a week before riding off to the next town.<br /> <br /> I could see ruling that a unit led in battle by a Solar for a while would maintain their enhanced traits even without the mote commitment if they continued to train and fight under her command.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805901#post805901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't have a problem with building cities single-handedly or personally re-writing the weather of an entire country with a sorcerous working or whatever. It just shouldn't be too quick.<br /> <br /> Exactly how quick is too quick is an open question. My gut instinct is that a Solar alone can be about as fast as 20 mortal masters, and that they can get things done much much faster than that when inspiring and leading a large group.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you explain why someone would want to build a city? I could imagine a powerful sorcerer showing off by pulling fully-formed buildings from the earth or warping living trees into homes and such. But in general I'd expect a Solar (like any other leader) to just let people build their own homes and businesses and farms as they see fit. Planning and organizing make sense, as does the Solar managing the construction of things she will use.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T04:57:22-08:00","id":805985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A horde of 1000 charges you, you kill 50 in short order, hundreds realize they're not being paid enough for this and flee. The battle group loses a point of Size. The next horde of 1000 says, &quot;what a bunch of cowards&quot; and charges.</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iu7vySQbgXI\" data-code=\"Iu7vySQbgXI\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/Iu7vySQbgXI/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T05:26:54-08:00","id":805996,"title":"","content":"Just remember, Isator - this is your 7,999th post. Make your next one amazing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T14:16:05-08:00","id":806457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Root</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805928#post805928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure. The duration is Indefinite, and there is no clause ending the mote commitment and making the effects permanent as there is for League of Iron Preparation. That seems to suggest that the Tiger Warriors are the Exalt's personal army, not a group of people she trained for a week before riding off to the next town.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh, never noticed the duration. But I <i>think</i> the effect is meant to outlast the commitment.<br /> <br /> I mean, I'd assume it works the same way as LoIP even if they're phrased differently. And it'd be lame if leading Tiger Warriors into battle left you 10 motes behind the guy leading regular elite troops.<br /> <br /> All the really supernatural permanent effects cost xp, so it wouldn't be broken for it to be permanent.<br /> <br /> Then again, maybe I'm just seeing what I want to see. It's at times like these that I wish they hadn't tried to use natural language for the Charms...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Root</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805928#post805928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you explain why someone would want to build a city? I could imagine a powerful sorcerer showing off by pulling fully-formed buildings from the earth or warping living trees into homes and such. But in general I'd expect a Solar (like any other leader) to just let people build their own homes and businesses and farms as they see fit. Planning and organizing make sense, as does the Solar managing the construction of things she will use.</div> </div> </div> </div>So that people can live in it, of course. Maybe they've gotta pay, maybe they've gotta be loyal, maybe it's charity.<br /> <br /> Or maybe the whole thing is just art.<br /> <br /> Regardless, doing it yourself should be possible but slow. There should be strong incentives to get help.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T14:29:22-08:00","id":806469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806457#post806457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Huh, never noticed the duration. But I <i>think</i> the effect is meant to outlast the commitment.<br /> <br /> I mean, I'd assume it works the same way as LoIP even if they're phrased differently. And it'd be lame if leading Tiger Warriors into battle left you 10 motes behind the guy leading regular elite troops.<br /> <br /> All the really supernatural permanent effects cost xp, so it wouldn't be broken for it to be permanent.<br /> <br /> Then again, maybe I'm just seeing what I want to see. It's at times like these that I wish they hadn't tried to use natural language for the Charms...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So that people can live in it, of course. Maybe they've gotta pay, maybe they've gotta be loyal, maybe it's charity.<br /> <br /> Or maybe the whole thing is just art.<br /> <br /> Regardless, doing it yourself should be possible but slow. There should be strong incentives to get help.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does this help?<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/737191-indefinite-mote-commitment\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/737191-indefinite-mote-commitment\"> Indefinite Mote Commitment - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/737191-indefinite-mote-commitment\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/737191-indefinite-mote-commitment </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Indefinite mote commitment cant possibly work the way it claims in the book: for the purpose of training npcs. For reference: &ldquo;With the exception of permanent </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Exalted: Princes of the Universe<br /> A Third Edition Exalted Podcast<br /> <b><u><a href=\"http://atrusilk.podbean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://atrusilk.podbean.com/</a></u></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8769-tavelgorge","name":"TavelGorge","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"498"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T15:27:17-08:00","id":806518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806457#post806457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, I'd assume it works the same way as LoIP even if they're phrased differently. And it'd be lame if leading Tiger Warriors into battle left you 10 motes behind the guy leading regular elite troops.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, I guess we'll see the intent soon. To me it seems a little incongruous to let the Solar bond with these troops, shape them into one of the greatest fighting forces in Creation, and then just walk away.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806457#post806457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There should be strong incentives to get help.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with this last part, and I think the current system does that. A Solar might lead her followers to the promised land and try to set them on a certain path. But why would she build everything they need? Why even consider it, when there are already people who can build houses? Would she also cook every meal, forge every tool, and sew every garment, or is that where it turns silly?<br /> <br /> If she wants wealth, applying her industriousness to something else can bring her more than she'll get by turning into some kind of Bronze Age real estate mogul anyway. Again this feels incongruous - you've got a champion of heaven's mightiest god personally building homes for dozens or hundreds of families. Writing architectural manuals makes sense, or teaching builders a few new things is fine, but not stacking stone upon stone thousands of times. Teach a man to fish, right?<br /> <br /> Similarly, Solars should be devising codes of laws and appointing magistrates, not personally arbitrating every dispute by using a charm to let everyone negotiate at super-speed. Solars write books and send their priests out to educate people - they don't go house to house teaching each child to read, nor do they round them all up and magically invest them with literacy. Their charms are appropriate to their roles in defining, protecting, sustaining, and advancing a civilization, but not building one from the ground up while mortals stand around and watch.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T16:01:43-08:00","id":806544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Root</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806518#post806518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm, I guess we'll see the intent soon. To me it seems a little incongruous to let the Solar bond with these troops, shape them into one of the greatest fighting forces in Creation, and then just walk away.<br /> <br /> Similarly, Solars should be devising codes of laws and appointing magistrates, not personally arbitrating every dispute by using a charm to let everyone negotiate at super-speed. Solars write books and send their priests out to educate people - they don't go house to house teaching each child to read, nor do they round them all up and magically invest them with literacy. Their charms are appropriate to their roles in defining, protecting, sustaining, and advancing a civilization, but not building one from the ground up while mortals stand around and watch.</div> </div> </div> </div>There actually were Solars in 2nd edition that would travel from town to town, training people to defend themselves, and then leave. At least in 2nd edition, Tiger Warrior Training did not automatically turn people into elite troops- if all you had was a couple of weeks, you could raise their Drill and Abilities slightly and then say &quot;gotta go&quot; and leave them to where they could at least handle the local bandits. I know that's not the exact same thing as making elite troops everywhere you go, but there should definitely be a level where you can use your head and your charms to do something with a benefit that lasts beyond your stay in the location and no longer depends on your power. I guess finding the proper balance is the real trick, and I wonder how E3's doing in that regard.<br /> <br /> In regards to building infrastructure single-handly, I think you're right, but I believe a Solar master builder could also take some kind of middle ground- do something spectacular that only he or she could do, pass along a couple of other designs that mortals can actually perform to help or maintain afterwards, and then trust them and the super-project to carry the community onward.<br /> <br /> An example that comes to my mind is the Prism, a super-builder mage in the Lightbringer book series by Brent Weeks. VAGUE SPOILER: Faced with an oncoming invasion, he quickly designs a super defense for a city, which has several components only he can perform. But he instructs everyone else on how to build the other components, and they do whatever they can to support him in the project (providing him with magic resources, doing some small magic themselves, offering small suggestions to his design).<br /> <br /> In another location, he builds another defense, then shares a schematic showing how it can be repaired over time (BORING SPOILER: he shows them how to make special bricks for it).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3752-mushroom-king","name":"Mushroom King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3752_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"221"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T17:34:14-08:00","id":806602,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806544#post806544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There actually were Solars in 2nd edition that would travel from town to town, training people to defend themselves, and then leave.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, can't a Solar still do so? I wouldn't want to have to buy a particular charm to be able to say that the farmers in a few villages can fight back OK and have some plans for defending themselves. For bigger threats it makes sense that the Solar is more personally involved. Given some time and the right resources, it's fair to assume she can prepare people to handle some bandits. Supernatural menaces and Realm legions take more work though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mushroom King</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806544#post806544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In regards to building infrastructure single-handly, I think you're right, but I believe a Solar master builder could also take some kind of middle ground- do something spectacular that only he or she could do, pass along a couple of other designs that mortals can actually perform to help or maintain afterwards, and then trust them and the super-project to carry the community onward.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's the stuff that makes sense. The Solar should be designing and leading the construction of aqueducts, roads, walls, and temples, not stone huts and goat pens.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-11T22:58:03-08:00","id":806791,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=805646#post805646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, there's a point at which one person or five people fighting tens of thousands just looks silly rather than impressive to me; the bad fight choreography of &quot;the ninjas surround the hero and then politely go in one at a time and roll out of the way after a single punch&quot; taking to a ludicrous scale, a scale where you've got some of those ninjas <i>miles</i> away from the individual they're standing in line to be punched down by.</div> </div> </div> </div>One guy fighting an army has mythological representation in the sources Exalted draws on. Samson beat an army with the jawbone of an ox. Then there's the story of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horatius_Cocles\" target=\"_blank\">Horatius Cocles</a>, who supposedly (with two other soldiers) held the bridge to Rome against an entire army.<br /> <br /> Having a chokepoint and standing against an army determined to go through it helps make it a bit more reasonable, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 01-12-2016, 02:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-12T00:55:31-08:00","id":806840,"title":"","content":"Like I said earlier, it's totally thematic and awesome for a single Solar or an entire Circle to beat an army without help. Likewise, it's thematic for a Solar to judge his subjects in the vein of Solomon, find them food in the desert like Moses, and build mighty cities with impenetrable walls, awesome sanitation and a palace of unbelievable splendor for himself in the midst of it, like every ruler worth his salt at least wanted to do.<br /> <br /> What isn't exactly thematic is the Solar personally overseeing the digging of every latrine in his city, judging every minor marital quibble in his court, providing food for his subjects constantly in lieu of them working, and making himself the only defense his kingdom ever gets. Because, c'mon, people don't work that way. States don't work that way.<br /> <br /> Exceptions apply, sorta - if, say, a powerful Solar Sorcerer wants to create a sorcerous utopia, it's viable for him to establish Workings that'd make his state the Greek Golden Age, with fields plowing themselves, food appearing magically on people's tables and whatnot. But that's still a form of delegating responsibility, albeit you delegate it to magical constructs and bound spirit slaves and such rather than to humans. And I'd still expect him to raise an army at some point, even if it's made up of automata and bound spirits, rather than Death Raying every approaching army ever - you don't establish a magical utopia to remain the one person slaving away all the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-12T13:49:40-08:00","id":807272,"title":"","content":"I disagree. Nothing is below a Solar.<br /> <br /> One of the few things I actually liked about the Craft system is that it didn't put Solars above digging toilets.<br /> <br /> Solars aren't paragons of upper-middle-class excellence. They're paragons of human excellence. If your Solar is a peasant, they can stay a peasant, and be the Greatest Of All Peasants. If they want to.<br /> <br /> Sure, they can't dig every latrine and judge every quibble and grow all the food and fight all the wars. But they could do any one of those things. Building a city wall alone, like the builder that the Aesir hired, is totally in-theme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TavelGorge</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=806469#post806469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does this help?<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/737191-indefinite-mote-commitment\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...ote-commitment</a></div> </div> </div> </div>It does. I'm pretty confident in my original take now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\" target=\"_blank\">EX3 Craft Rewrite</a><br /> <br /> Sanctaphrax is not a person<br /> -Chejop Kejak","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1154-sanctaphrax","name":"Sanctaphrax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1154_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T00:27:19-08:00","id":807681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=807272#post807272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree. Nothing is below a Solar.<br /> <br /> One of the few things I actually liked about the Craft system is that it didn't put Solars above digging toilets.<br /> <br /> Solars aren't paragons of upper-middle-class excellence. They're paragons of human excellence. If your Solar is a peasant, they can stay a peasant, and be the Greatest Of All Peasants. If they want to.<br /> <br /> Sure, they can't dig every latrine and judge every quibble and grow all the food and fight all the wars. But they could do any one of those things. Building a city wall alone, like the builder that the Aesir hired, is totally in-theme.</div> </div> </div> </div>No particular task is beneath a Solar, perhaps. That doesn't mean their charms should be structured to facilitate building every little house.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't find much useful guidance in the idea of Solars as generically excellent. That leads to charms for everything, since excellence is always possible and theoretically desirable. When you narrow down to effects that make sense for the greatest heroes and leaders of the Exalted, you weed out a lot of stuff that makes the Solar into something else or that make her success in leadership a guarantee.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"color:#696969\">When all the world was very young<br /> And mountain magic heavy hung<br /> The supermen would walk in file<br /> Guardians of a loveless isle</span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2010-root","name":"Root","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1014"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T02:50:39-08:00","id":807746,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/804011-why-solars-can-t-change-the-setting?p=807272#post807272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree. Nothing is below a Solar.<br /> <br /> One of the few things I actually liked about the Craft system is that it didn't put Solars above digging toilets.<br /> <br /> Solars aren't paragons of upper-middle-class excellence. They're paragons of human excellence. If your Solar is a peasant, they can stay a peasant, and be the Greatest Of All Peasants. If they want to.<br /> <br /> Sure, they can't dig every latrine and judge every quibble and grow all the food and fight all the wars. But they could do any one of those things. Building a city wall alone, like the builder that the Aesir hired, is totally in-theme.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, I'm not disputing that it can be done.<br /> <br /> I'm saying it's neither reasonable nor effort-effective to do so. Especially in the context of being a ruler and enacting large-scale change upon the setting. Which is what we're discussing in this thread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}